    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.063.c1",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "A Roman poet said his people wanted only two things: bread and circuses."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.063.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Free grain",
      "body": "Rome gave free grain to many citizens living in the city. Under Augustus about 200,000 people received it. Much of that grain came from Egypt, which was under the emperor’s personal control."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.063.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Free games",
      "body": "Public games, ludi, began as religious festivals and grew into huge free entertainments. Emperors and politicians paid for games and food to win popularity and keep the city calm.",
      "callout": "Ludi: public games"
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.063.c4",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "“Bread and circuses” was a complaint.",
      "context": "The poet Juvenal said that the Roman people, who once handed out commands, now wanted only these two things. He meant that citizens had traded political power for free food and shows."
    },
    {
